> Display scaling has a measurable effect

A constant 2 channel beside a constant 4 channel appears half-bright under a shared
maximum 4. Per-channel normalization makes both white, and can therefore make a
weak response look as strong as a large one. Zero channels stay black under
either rule; constant positive channels must remain visible. Channel indices
are metadata and are never re-ranked to choose attractive maps.
```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/filter_activations_scaling.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <print>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  namespace m = n::models;
  const auto x = n::from_vector(std::vector<float>{0, 2, 0, 2, 0, 4, 0, 4}, {2, 1, 2, 2});
  const auto shared = m::VisualChannelImages(x, false),
             local = m::VisualChannelImages(x, true);
  std::print(
      "Declared observation-major/channel-minor shared images {},per-channel images {}.\n",
      shared.reshape({4, 2}), local.reshape({4, 2}));
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.points(n::arange(0, 4).to_vector<double>(),
              n::mean(shared, {1, 2, 3}).to_vector<double>(), "shared",
              {37, 99, 235})
      .points(n::arange(0, 4).to_vector<double>(),
              n::mean(local, {1, 2, 3}).to_vector<double>(), "local",
              {220, 38, 38})
      .title("Declared zero,weak and strong channels")
      .x_label("observation/channel image ordinal")
      .y_label("display intensity")
      .y_limits(0, 1.2);
  plot.save_svg("build/filter_activations_scaling.svg");
}
```
